/-
  Perfect — why trivial homology cannot recognise the 3-sphere.

  Clay: poincare meta

  The Poincaré conjecture, proved by Grigori Perelman (2002–2003) on paper and not formalised here,
  says that a closed 3-manifold with trivial fundamental group π₁ is the 3-sphere. Poincaré first
  asked it with trivial HOMOLOGY instead, and then found a counterexample: the Poincaré homology
  sphere has the homology of the 3-sphere but π₁ of order 120 (the binary icosahedral group). That is
  why the conjecture is stated with π₁. The first homology group H₁ is the abelianization of π₁, and
  an argument that used only homology would need "trivial abelianization" to imply "trivial group".
  This file shows that it does not, with the smallest example: the alternating group A5, a quotient of
  the binary icosahedral group. In A5 every element is a commutator, so the abelianization is trivial,
  and yet A5 has 60 elements.

  A permutation of {0, …, 4} is the list of its images. (p ∘ q)(i) = p(q(i)), and the commutator of
  a and b is a ∘ b ∘ a⁻¹ ∘ b⁻¹. The table lists every element g of A5 with a pair (a, b) in A5 whose
  commutator is g. The table was generated by search and is checked here by the kernel.

  What is proved, by `decide` alone:
    1. Every row's g is the commutator of its a and b.
    2. Every g, a and b in the table is an even permutation of {0, …, 4}, so all lie in A5.
    3. The table lists 60 distinct elements, so every element of A5 is a commutator: A5 equals its
       commutator subgroup and its abelianization is trivial.

  What is NOT proved: anything about 3-manifolds, or the Poincaré conjecture, which Perelman proved.
  The file shows only that a group with trivial abelianization can be large, so trivial homology does
  not certify trivial π₁, and an argument from homology alone cannot recognise the 3-sphere.
-/

namespace Perfect

/-- The n-th entry, 0 past the end. Structural recursion, so a `decide` over it rests on no axiom
    (the library's defaulting lookup carries propext). -/
def nth : List Nat → Nat → Nat
  | [], _ => 0
  | x :: _, 0 => x
  | _ :: xs, n + 1 => nth xs n

/-- (p ∘ q)(i) = p(q(i)). -/
def compose (p q : List Nat) : List Nat := (List.range 5).map (fun i => nth p (nth q i))

/-- p⁻¹(i): the position of i in p. -/
def inv (p : List Nat) : List Nat := (List.range 5).map (fun i => p.idxOf i)

/-- a ∘ b ∘ a⁻¹ ∘ b⁻¹. -/
def commutator (a b : List Nat) : List Nat := compose (compose a b) (compose (inv a) (inv b))

/-- A permutation of {0, …, 4}: five entries, each of 0 … 4, no repeats. -/
def isPerm (p : List Nat) : Bool := p.length == 5 && p.all (· < 5) && (List.range 5).all (fun i => p.contains i)

/-- The number of inversions. -/
def inversions (p : List Nat) : Nat :=
  (List.range 5).foldl (fun c i => (List.range 5).foldl (fun c j => if i < j && nth p i > nth p j then c + 1 else c) c) 0

/-- Even: an even number of inversions. -/
def even (p : List Nat) : Bool := isPerm p && inversions p % 2 == 0

